designs.angle:
INCREMENTAL, WHERE THE ROWS ARE. I leave the consumer hot loop (consume / consume2) and the ring as they are. On the H200 the v4 family already streams at 7.34 us per pass, 88% of the 4.37 TB/s ring ceiling. The gap at T = 2..32 is the fixed cost, about 20-21 us per call against a 9.3 us empty-launch floor. At T = 128/256 the decode cold half carries a 27.4 us intercept. The per-PC anatomy (P3b) shows where that fixed cost goes:
- the stream-K fix-up: 11.6-17.0% of v4 samples, of which MEMBAR is 6.7-9.7%;
- thread 0's serial build_map loop: 4.3-10.4%;
- the 64-bit split math: 0.5-1.4%;
- the epilogue's gs[le] global load: about 1.5%.

Each piece gets a bitwise-equal fix inside one new kernel pair (eak4x). The pair is raced as the twin of whatever v4-family engine each class routes today, so each class has one routable cell. It hits moe T=8/16/32/128 (stage 1) and then decode T=8/16/32 plus the hot/cold cold half at T=128/256 (stage 2). T=1 (v6pe) and the wgmma hot half are out of scope. The bigger remaining big-T gap is the wgmma hot half: 1.1-1.7 TB/s with a ~20 us intercept. Stage 3 only re-races the work split once v4x is cheaper.

designs.mechanism:
The base is rewrite/lib/cuda/ea4_kernels.cuh, identical in eb-trim and eb-trimd (509 lines). The new header ea4x_kernels.cuh holds namespace eak4x. It reuses eak4's MapS/Map, THREADS, NCW, CAP, D, STAGE2, XS, PART, CW1 and bar_consumers, plus eak::consume and eak3::consume2, unchanged.

UNCHANGED from v4
- Decomposition: the unit is (pass ≤ 16 routes, 128-row super-group, 512-k chunk).
- Aligned split when n_v·NS ≤ G; otherwise stream-K over grid 264 (2 CTAs/SM). v4c's cost weighting comes from pdl bits 4..7.
- Data movement: per stage, two 18,432 B weight records plus ≤ 16 x/h rows of 1 KB, D=2 ring. K2 prefetches weights before griddepcontrol.wait. K1 stays plain in decode.
- consume / consume2, the PDL placement, and the error word with bounded mbarrier waits.

THE FIVE EDITS (each bitwise-neutral)

(1) build_map_x replaces build_map_t. Steps 1-3 and 5 stay as in lines 58-101 and 117-126; step 4, thread 0's serial loop (lines 102-116), becomes warp 0 with lane = expert:
```
cnt = m.cnt[lane]; np = (cnt + 15) / 16;
last = np ? cnt - 16*(np-1) : 0;
cost = np ? (np-1)*cw + (last > 8 ? cw : CW1) : 0;
// inclusive __shfl_up_sync scans (5 rounds) of cnt, np, cost -> rb, rp, rc
base = rb - cnt; p0 = rp - np; c0 = rc - cost; m.base[lane] = base;
for p < np: v_e[p0+p] = lane; v_beg[p0+p] = base + 16p; v_n[p0+p] = (p+1 < np) ? 16 : last;
            if (cw) s_cost[p0+p] = c0 + p*cw;
lane 31: n_v = rp; n_local = rb; if (cw) s_cost[rp] = rc;
```
- cw = (pdl >> 4) & 15 is passed in. The weighted block in lines 169-182 (thread 0's s_cost loop and its __syncthreads) is deleted.
- In step 1, threads t < count also load s_gs[t] = gs[t]. s_gs is 32 floats placed after the Map.
- The serial loop lists passes by ascending expert, then by ascending p, so the Map is identical.

(2) 32-bit split. begin_of, owner_of, cost_of and the finisher's bc (lines 184-209, 323) use unsigned 32-bit arithmetic. Bounds:
- W ≤ 160·64·8 = 81,920;
- CT ≤ 160·15·512 = 1,228,800;
- G ≤ 512 (host TORCH_CHECK);
- largest product 629,145,600 < 2^31, enforced by static_assert.

(3) Deferred tickets. The fix-up block in lines 299-347 changes from 'store, then per-warp __threadfence (MEMBAR.SC.GPU), bar, thread-0 atomicAdd, bar; finisher __threadfence, then serial __ldcg' to the following.

At a partial super-group end, the consumers only store their partial into ws[(blockIdx.x·2 + slot_mine)·PART] (tile 1 when two). slot_mine = (sgrp == b/NK) ? 0 : 1, as today. Record pend0 or pend1 = sgrp (CTA-uniform) and continue with no fence, no barrier and no ticket. Complete super-groups run the epilogue as before. After the loop:
```
if (pend0 >= 0 || pend1 >= 0) {
  bar_consumers();
  if (threadIdx.x == 0) {
    asm volatile("fence.acq_rel.gpu;" ::: "memory");   // release, cumulative over the CTA's stores via bar.sync
    o0 = atomicAdd(&tickets[tk(pend0)], 1); o1 = atomicAdd(&tickets[tk(pend1)], 1);   // each only if pending; both in flight
    asm volatile("fence.acq_rel.gpu;" ::: "memory");   // acquire
    m.flag = (o0 == nc(pend0) - 1) | ((o1 == nc(pend1) - 1) << 1);
  }
  bar_consumers();
  if (m.flag & 1) finish(pend0);
  if (m.flag & 2) finish(pend1);
}
```
- tk(s) = v·NS + si.
- nc(s) = owner_of(g0 + NK - 1) - owner_of(g0) + 1.
- finish(s) recomputes c_first, c_last and first_s1 exactly as lines 310 and 321-324. It issues the contributors' __ldcg loads four at a time (predicated, with a loop beyond four), adds them in CTA order from 0.f (the same fp32 order as today), and thread 0 resets the ticket to 0. It then runs the epilogue.
- The epilogue (lines 348-376) becomes a lambda shared by complete and finished super-groups.
- This is the CUTLASS GenericBarrier ordering (barrier.h arrive_inc / wait_eq). hypotheses/LESSONS.md:48-50 records the same pattern (barrier, one gpu-scope acq_rel ticket, winner reloads with .cg, fixed-order sum) passing correctness on the judge.

(4) gs from smem. The epilogue reads sc = s_gs[le] instead of the global gs[le] at line 351.

(5) Shared memory. EA4X_SMEM = EA4_SMEM + 128 = 113,136 B, and 2·(113,136 + 1,024) = 228,320 ≤ 233,472, so two CTAs per SM still fit (static_assert).

What moves: the mid-stream head stall goes away entirely (today: 8 warps' MEMBAR.SC, 2 barriers and an atomic round trip). The remaining end work costs 1 release, 1 round trip for both tickets, 1 acquire and a batched-load round trip, where today it costs 2-3 rounds of 8-warp MEMBAR.SC, the serialized ticket round trips and the finisher's serial loads. The finish moves to the later-ending contributor, so a CTA finishes 0-2 super-groups, 1 on average. Thread 0's ~1,600-cycle serial loop becomes ~250 cycles. One v4c __syncthreads, the 64-bit divides and one L2 round trip per super-group end disappear.

MODULE
- ea4x_core.py builds okc_campaign_engine_a_v4x_<sha10> in a daemon thread from prepare() entry, modelled on _ea6_build_start/_ea6_build_join (ea6_core.py:74-120).
- Source = _EA2_SRC's #include lines + the ea_common.cuh section + the namespace slices eak (ea_kernels.cuh), eak2 (ea2_kernels.cuh:12-306), eak3 (ea3_kernels.cuh:15-328) and eak4 (ea4_kernels.cuh:19-396), like _ea6_source, + #@include lib/cuda/ea4x_kernels.cuh.
- ea4x_kernels.cuh carries its own host code: ea4x_info, plus ea4x_w13 / ea4x_w2 with ea4_w13/ea4_w2's signatures, dq == 2 only, its own TORCH_CHECKs and the cudaFuncSetAttribute call.
- The board's Engine A module is untouched, so a v4x compile error costs nothing. Join by EA4X_JOIN_S = 170 s.

GATING (state set in prepare, kernel_fn branches on shape and state only)
- New engines "v4x" and "v4cx" (cw = EA4C_CW = 7). Their cores are _ea3_forward_core with kern=("ea4x_w13", "ea4x_w2") on ea["ext4x"]: add an ext_key argument, default "ext".
- Admission: v4x occupancy == 2, spill == 0, regs ≤ 112.
- Bitwise canary against the class's incumbent. h and y are NaN-poisoned (a poison flag in the core, as v6's canary does). Every T in the routed classes is run against _ea_routings' kinds (zipf, random, local8, skewed-4hot, one-local, two-local, zero-local, max-active), plus T=128 judge draws p10/p50/p90, plus forced aligned s=2/4 cases (T=2 one-local / two-local). Each run is repeated twice, error word 0.
- Race per class: cells 8, 16, 32 and 128. It compares the incumbent (pins kept) against its twin: v4→v4x, v4c→v4cx.
  - moe: kernel_fn graphs on _combo_routings (zipf, random) at T=cls; T=128 uses _ea_judge_routings. _rt_time read flush, 2 rounds; _rt_select margin 1.01, tight 1.005.
  - decode (stage 2): _ead_layer_gate whole layer, margin 1.005, tight 1.003. The cold halves race through hotcold's layer gate with a cold_x flag per class.
- The existing copy checks run after these races, so they cover the twins.
- PATH lines name the twin, for example "T=2..16 = ENGINE A v4x (twin of v4 PINNED)". A compact [ea4x] block of ≤ 6 lines × 199 chars is printed last in prepare (the 14 KB log-tail lesson).

